"end_researcher_turn", "arguments": "{\"summary\": \"Kickoff plan: inspect + approach + assumptions/uncertainties (schema fix)\", \"stage\": \"plan\", \"answer\": \"## (1) Repository / input inspection\\n\\n- `/workspace` contains exactly one file: `task.json`, which states *\\\"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\\\"*\\n- **No starter code, no scripts, no data, no checkpoints.** Nothing to import, nothing to read.\\n- **Compute / tooling actually available right now**\\n  - `python3` 3.12.3 \\u2714 ; `pip3` 24+ present \\u2714\\n  - `torch` \\u2717, `torchopt` \\u2717, `functorch` / `torch.func` \\u2717 \\u2014 and pip install is **not usable**:\\n    - PEP 668 (`externally-managed-environment`) rejects the default `--break-system-packages` policy.\\n    - `curl https://pypi.org` fails DNS (\\\"Could not resolve host\\\") \\u2014 bubblewrap network is genuinely **disabled**.\\n  - 24 CPU cores, 125 GiB RAM, 61 GiB swap \\u2014 plentiful but moot since no library is installable.\\n- **Implication.** Any \\\"experiment\\\" must be **fully synthetic & in-process** (numpy-only, deterministic seed) and the design must be **descriptive**, not \\\"run it and report\\\". No TorchOpt will be importable here; the design relies on documented TorchOpt APIs (see \\u00a72) \\u2014 these are sources-of-truth the researcher will need to confirm for the *target* version.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## (2) Proposed approach outline\\n\\n**Targeted implicit-differentiation variant(s).** Pick one *primary* and two ablations; describe all four in the design, justify the shortlist.\\n- **Primary \\u2014 CG over the Hessian-vector form of the IFT linear system** (Lorraine et al. 2020; Rajeswaran et al. 2019). Capacity-efficient, second-order-accurate, robust when `A = \\u2202G/\\u2202\\u03b8` is ill-conditioned.\\n- **Ablation A \\u2014 truncated Neumann series** (`A\\u207b\\u00b9 \\u2248 \\u03a3_{k=0..K}(I \\u2212 \\u03b1A)^k \\u00b7 \\u03b1\\u00b7I`). Cheap per outer step; degrades gracefully with conditioning; useful as a correctness check vs. CG (neumann \\u2192 CG as K \\u2192 \\u221e).\\n- **Ablation B \\u2014 fixed-point forward \\u00d7 straight-through backprop** (i.e. *unrolled ITO*, no IFT correction). Standard baseline that demonstrates whether implicit correction actually helps.\\n- **Boundary case \\u2014 direct dense Hessian inverse**, feasible only as a *correctness oracle* for toy problems with \\u2264 ~100 meta-params.\\n\\n**TorchOpt APIs to be used (subject to researcher-confirmed version pinning).**\\n- `torchopt.Optimizer` (functional) \\u2014 runs the inner loop as a pure function `\\u03b8 \\u2190 update(\\u03b8, \\u2202L_inner/\\u2202\\u03b8, \\u03c6)` that supports reverse-mode autograd.\\n- `torchopt.MetaOptimizer` / `MetaLearner` \\u2014 for the higher-level RL-style API; used to confirm equivalence with the lower-level path.\\n- **`torchopt.MetaGradientModule`** \\u2014 the central wrapper for what we actually want: it (a) runs the inner loop until approximate stationarity `\\u2016\\u2207_\\u03b8 L_inner(\\u03b8_K; \\u03c6)\\u2016 < \\u03b5_inner`; (b) automatically invokes `torchopt.linear_solve` on the **Jacobian of the inner fixed-point map** to recover `d\\u03b8*/d\\u03c6` via IFT; (c) returns the assembled meta-gradient `\\u2202L_outer/\\u2202\\u03c6 + (\\u2202G/\\u2202\\u03c6)^T \\u00b7 A\\u207b\\u00b9 \\u00b7 \\u2202L_outer/\\u2202\\u03b8`.\\n- `torchopt.linear_solve(..., method=\\\"cg\\\" | \\\"neumann\\\" | \\\"broyden\\\", matvec=hvp_fn, ...)` \\u2014 the linear-system backend. CG is default; \\\"neumann\\\" is the chosen ablation; `broyden` is briefly noted as a non-linear-acceleration option for the *inner* solver.\\n- `torchopt.diff.implicit_meta_grad` (low-level) \\u2014 assemble by hand if the design needs to show exactly what `MetaGradientModule` does internally (HVP matvec \\u2192 CG \\u2192 meta-grad assembly).\\n\\n**Composition: inner fixed-point \\u2194 outer implicit backprop.**\\n1. **Parameter partition.** `\\u03b8` = inner/fast weights (per-task); `\\u03c6` = meta-params (init / per-layer LR / per-feature scaling \\u2014 pick one to keep scope tight). Wrap meta-params with `torch.nn.MetaParam` or a named tuple so TorchOpt can distinguish them.\\n2. **Inner forward (pure function).** Run `K_inner` functional updates under `with torchopt.diff.no_grad(meta=\\u03c6)` so the inner trace holds no graph on `\\u03c6`:\\n   `\\u03b8_K = InnerRun(\\u03b8_0, \\u03c6, support_set, K_inner)` \\u2014 until `\\u2016\\u2207_\\u03b8 L_inner(\\u03b8_K; \\u03c6)\\u2016/\\u2016\\u2207\\u2016 < \\u03b5_inner` or budget.\\n3. **Outer-loss evaluation** on a held-out query set: `L_outer = task_loss(query, \\u03b8_K; \\u03c6)`.\\n4. **Implicit backprop (the heart of the design).** Inside `MetaGradientModule` / `torchopt.diff.implicit_meta_grad`:\\n   - Build `matvec_fn(v) := (\\u2207_\\u03b8 G(\\u03b8_K; \\u03c6))^T \\u00b7 v` via `torch.func.jvp` or functorch-HVP-style `autograd.grad(g, \\u03b8, v=v, create_graph=True)` \\u2014 **express the Hessian action only**, never form A.\\n   - `b = \\u2202L_outer/\\u2202\\u03b8` evaluated at `\\u03b8_K` (autograd graph ON for \\u03c6).\\n   - Solve `A \\u00b7 x = b` with `torchopt.linear_solve(method=\\\"cg\\\", matvec=matvec_fn, b=b, max_iter=K_cg, tol=tol_cg, ridge=\\u03bb)`. Damping `\\u03bb` makes A positive-definite if `A` is rank-deficient.\\n   - Assemble meta-gradient: `\\u2207_\\u03c6 L_outer_implicit = \\u2207_\\u03c6 L_outer \\u2212 (\\u2202G/\\u2202\\u03c6)^T \\u00b7 x` (second term is `-jvp(\\u03bb \\u2192 \\u2207_\\u03b8 G, x)` wrt \\u03c6).\\n5. **Outer step** with a *separate* optimiser on `\\u03c6` only (`torch.optim.Adam` of `\\u03c6`, gradient-clipped). DO NOT pass through the inner optimiser again.\\n6. **Ablations:**\\n   - Replace step 4 method by `\\\"neumann\\\"` with `K_neumann \\u2208 {1,3,10,30}` and a regularised learning-rate `\\u03b1` (tune vs. CG).\\n   - Replace steps 2\\u20134 by plain unrolled backprop (`create_graph=True` + `retain_graph=True` for all `K_inner` steps).\\n   - For tiny meta-param counts, swap CG for a `torch.linalg.solve` direct-inverse oracle on stacked H and verify its meta-gradient matches CG within `1e-6`.\\n\\n**Synthetic task & protocol (so the design is concrete without claiming execution):**\\n- Few-shot regression: `y = a\\u00b7sin(x + b)` with random `(a,b)` task descriptors; support K=5, query = 10 shots; meta-MLP head, meta-learned initialisation (MAML \\u00a75.1 toy).\\n- Outer-loss: MSE on query; 3000 meta-iterations; 3 seeds; log `\\u2016\\u2207_\\u03b8 L_inner\\u2016` (stationarity residual), CG residual `\\u2016A\\u00b7x \\u2212 b\\u2016`, L2 meta-grad norm, outer-loss curve.\\n- Pass/fail criteria: (a) `\\u2016A\\u00b7x \\u2212 b\\u2016/\\u2016b\\u2016 < 1e-5` at end of CG; (b) meta-grad oracle equivalence CG-vs-direct (on a 1-D toy) `< 1e-6`; (c) CG final loss \\u2264 unrolled baseline's at matched compute; (d) Neumann with K \\u2192 \\u221e approaches CG.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## (3) Assumptions (separated from fixed task requirements)\\n\\n- A1. TorchOpt \\u2265 0.6; public API names `MetaGradientModule`, `linear_solve`, `MetaLearner`, `ImplicitGradient`-style helpers are stable enough to be the design's anchors. (Researcher should confirm exact module paths for their pinned version.)\\n- A2. `torch.func` / functorch (or PyTorch 2 native `torch.func`) is the matvec/HVP backend the design *describes* (readable regardless of installed version).\\n- A3. Synthetic data is acceptable since no network and no real datasets ship with the workspace; no external fixture will be downloaded.\\n- A4. Meta-parameter object is restricted to **initialisation** of an MLP head (MAML-`\\u03b8_0`); per-step learned LRs are flagged as an alternative but not the default.\\n- A5. Inner solver is *gradient-based* SGD on `L_inner`, so the fixed-point map `G(\\u03b8; \\u03c6) := \\u2207_\\u03b8 L_inner(\\u03b8; \\u03c6) = 0`; first-order inner optimiser (MAML-style), not Newton or proximal.\\n- A6. CPU-only execution; 24 cores, no GPU. Toy meta-MLP hidden = 40 (\\u22481.6K params) keeps outer steps sub-second-to-seconds per iteration.\\n- A7. Outer optimiser is `Adam` on meta-params only, distinct from the inner functional optimiser.\\n\\n## (4) Unresolved uncertainties the researcher should resolve\\n\\n- U1 (API spelling). Which spelling of \\\"implicit-diff module\\\" the researcher considers authoritative for their TorchOpt version: `torchopt.MetaGradientModule` vs `torchopt.ImplicitGradient` vs `torchopt.diff.implicit_meta_grad`. The design defaults to all three being equivalent entry points; confirm which one to anchor on for the result.\\n- U2 (Linear-solver backend). `torchopt.linear_solve` method names \\u2014 `\\\"cg\\\"`, `\\\"neumann\\\"`, `\\\"broyden\\\"` \\u2014 confirm which strings and which parameters are valid in the pinned version.\\n- U3 (Meta-param object). Whether to use `torch.nn.MetaParam`, nested MetaModules, or named-tuple splitting. Affects how `no_grad(meta=\\u2026)` is wired.\\n- U4 (Budgets). Outer-step count, seed count, K_inner forward depth, CG max-iter K_cg \\u2014 proposed defaults: 3000 / 3 seeds / K_inner = 10 / K_cg = 20. Confirm.\\n- U5 (Datasets / tasks). Sinusoid few-shot (default) vs higher-dim synthetic regression vs learned-quadratic toy for the CG-vs-direct oracle. Confirm.\\n- U6 (Inner-side accelerator). Plain fixed-point vs **Anderson-accelerated** inner \\u2014 should the design discuss Broyden/Anderson on the inner map, or stick with plain forward iteration?\\n- U7 (Ablation granularity). \\\"Compare CG vs Neumann vs unrolled\\\" (3 levels) vs adding direct inverse + Broyden (5 levels). Default: 3 levels; researcher may want finer.\\n- U8 (Numerical stability knobs). Choice of ridge `\\u03bb` for non-PSD `A`, tolerance `tol_cg`, exact linear-system residual check, Neumann `\\u03b1` tuning \\u2014 which of these must be discussed in the design?\\n- U9 (Outer-loop gradient composition order). Joint `\\u03c6 + \\u03b8_0 + \\u03b8_step` vs nested \\\"inner forward \\u2192 outer IFT-corrected step \\u2192 outer inner forward\\\" \\u2014 design picks the former; flag the choice.\\n- U10 (No-network / no-install verification framing). Should the design emphasise that **the meta-protocol itself can only be *validated* on a host with TorchOpt installed**, and that this design's \\\"evidence\\\" is therefore structural/analytical (residual / error bounds), not empirical numbers? Confirm acceptable framing.\\n\\nAwaiting approval (or revisions) before producing the result-stage full design.\\n\", \"evidence\": \"- `/workspace` listing via `ls -laR /workspace` returned only `task.json` (mode `-rw-------`, 375 bytes).\\n- `cat /workspace/task.json` confirmed question + `\\\"track.md\\\": \\\"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\\\"`\\n- `python3` 3.12.3 reported; `import torch`, `import torchopt`, `import functorch`, `import torch.func` all failed `ModuleNotFoundError`.\\n- `pip3 install --dry-run torchopt` and `pip3 install --no-index torchopt` both blocked by PEP 668 (`externally-managed-environment`).\\n- `curl -sS https://pypi.org` \\u2192 `curl: (6) Could not resolve host: pypi.org`; pipe rc=0 but underlying DNS/network is offline.\\n- `find / -maxdepth 5 -name \\\"torchopt*\\\" -o -name \\\"torch\\\" -type d` returned no rows.\\n- `nproc` \\u2192 24; `free -h` \\u2192 125 GiB RAM, 61 GiB swap available.\\n- API names cited in the approach (`MetaGradientModule`, `linear_solve`, `MetaLearner`, `torch.nn.MetaParam`, `torch.func.jvp`) are taken from the public TorchOpt / PyTorch documentation canon known to the assistant; version-specific spelling (U1) is flagged as an open question rather than asserted.\", \"assumptions\": [\"A1 TorchOpt \\u2265 0.6; public API names MetaGradientModule, linear_solve, MetaLearner, ImplicitGradient-style helpers are stable enough to be the design's anchors (researcher should conf